diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index f1e2a49..3094ef0 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -527,6 +527,8 @@ HDMI tiles work the same way. Hold **OK** on one to **Rename** it — TV, PlaySt
 
 Yes. Pick the **Settings** tile, then **Wallpaper**. (**TV settings**, right under it, opens the TV's own settings.) There are fourteen gradients. Dark: Dusk (the default), Midnight, Plum, Forest, Graphite, Ocean and Ember. Bright: Sunset, Lagoon, Aurora, Coral, Iris, Meadow and Gold. The background changes as you move through the list; press **OK** to keep one, or **Back** to leave it as it was. They're colours only, no pictures, so every one is just as fast and uses the same memory.
 
+There is a fifteenth, **Wii**, which changes the whole page rather than just the colours: a white-to-pale-blue background, light grey tiles in a grid of four columns by three rows with each app's own icon (the start of the next page peeks in at the right edge, with a blue arrow over it), and a blue ribbon across the bottom that dips to a point in the middle. Above the ribbon is the time in a bold serif with a small italic am/pm (following your 12/24-hour setting); below it, the date spelled out ("Lunes, 16 de Noviembre de 2026"). Two big round buttons sit in the corners on grey plates, **Play Store** on the left and the **mail** envelope on the right (it opens Gmail, with a short message if the TV has none), each with a smaller one beside it: the **gear**, which opens the TV's settings, and a **bell**, which asks the system to open its notifications panel (most TVs have none and it says so). Along the top, the **Wi-Fi** icon (with the live download and upload rate under it) and, to its left, a **mobile signal** icon that shows no SIM on a TV and the real bars on a device that has one; and, top left, an **avatar** circle that opens the system's Google settings if it has them. On that wallpaper **hold OK (or press Menu) on the gear** for the home screen's own menu, which is where **Wallpaper** is, to go back to another one. Moving, replacing and removing apps, HDMI names and **Add app** all work as before; moving an app past the edge of a page carries it onto the next. It is all drawn in code; the avatar is a small picture kept as text in `AvatarData.java` (replace it with your own, or empty it for none). The fonts are the closest ones every TV has; to use others, such as Algerian and Harlow Solid Italic if you have the right to, put them in `res/raw/` as `clock_font.ttf` (the time) and `date_font.ttf` (the date).
+
 </details>
 
